  "scope_id": "FOUR-PREFER-20261101-001",
  "title": "Every failing four-place law builds equality",
  "subject": "Which four-place knots, including those that prefer a value, can have recognisable failures on 4-regular systems",
  "conclusion": "Of the 20 classes of four-place knots, every class that fails on small 4-regular systems builds a link (directly or by lifting a three-point core that realizes the twelve class) and is NP-complete on 4-regular systems, while parity's count is polynomial and the clause and not-all-equal never fail. So, unless NP-complete problems are polynomial, a mutually selecting four-place knot would have to be one of five weak classes, none of which failed in any search.",
  "authority_class": "proof",
